You missed the irony; I actually disagree with David in this instance 
(and he agrees with you).

Since the point is raised, I would like to be clear: I don't give a 
flying rip what standards other people play by. Savescum, startscum, 
play in wizmode and give yourself a one-turn ascension for all I care. 
(I've done it before. It's an amusing diversion.) Take all the notes you 
want. Pudding farm, rider farm, farm anything you want. I don't care how 
anyone else plays the game.

But in a discussion regarding what different players think is valid or 
invalid play, I'll weigh in with my opinion just like anyone else. And 
I'm not going to pepper my comments with IMO and YMMV; it's perfectly 
obvious that everything an individual poster has to offer is merely his 
or her own opinion and others are free to disagree.

But I fail to understand why, in discussions of this type, the person 
who advocates a style of play that makes the game a bit more difficult, 
rather than easier, is invariably told at some point that his opinion is 
invalid: for example, he may be told that he is "setting arbitrary 
standards for others" and needs to mind "his own damn business." (I've 
noticed, though, that this hostile response often takes the place of an 
actual rebuttal to the points that the other person has made.)

I've been able to have perfectly cordial conversations with others in 
this group over issues concerning which we disagree. I often find it 
stimulating and interesting to discover the reasoning behind conclusions 
that differ from my own. It's a pity that that can't be the case here.

